re working with amblin and they  brought an industrial Light Magic to us  and we thought okay we have this amazing  way of taking the audience to Mars in a  way that's never been done before  through these visual effects and then uh  Jess got access to the archive that NASA  had shot which was almost a thousand  hours of footage so usually we're verite  filmmakers meaning we're following  something unfolding but we thought even  though this story opportunity had  already had her beginning middle and end  through that Earthbound story and the  archival and then the visual effects on  Mars which were going to be photoreal  and based on all the real photographs  and data that we might be able to take  the audience on that Journey day by day  year by year instead of it being  retrospective what we didn't know was  who were the who were the human beings  that were going to be in the movie and I  I think if we're being honest we thought  that that was going to be the biggest  challenge we had this preconceived  notion that scientists and Engineers  maybe wouldn't be the most emotional  storytellers and we ended up being  completely wrong and Jess actually began  pre-interviewing all these people  including Jennifer and Doug and  everybody you know we only have 11 human  beings in our film because you can only  fit so many but there were dozens of  incredible people that we talked to who  all had their own singular individual  bond with this Rover their own anecdotes  of how it went side by side in their  life and so really I think the film  became all about the humanity Behind  These Rovers because the rovers are just  an extension of these of these human  beings I mean the the people the humans  in this are so fascinating and Jennifer  and Doug I'm curious when you first  heard about this project is this is this  the sort of thing that you would  normally do or was it specifically  because this story was so special that  you wanted to be a part of it  we missions like th