vies there's no money  but this from the very beginning stages  we all got to kind of throw in what we  needed  um so we began planning that but then  the this idea came to me that  I mean they're they're like children The  Rovers you know hearing their stories  through the whole thing they're they're  like these  these two Fragile Kids up there in cold  lonely space and I just thought you know  what's more lonely than just like one  piano note and that's how the score  opens so that was the idea and forgive  me because music is not my area of  expertise but it feels like each of the  Rovers kind of has their own theme um  and maybe even their own instruments  yeah they they have I mean very  different personalities right so they  they got their own themes um Doug didn't  get his own personal theme but yet  but what but what I did do with this  movie is uh I I felt like I had two  roles one was to to make you as Mark  will say um make you care about these  boxes of bolts and wires so it was this  fine line of how much do you push the  emotion because at some point you're  like that's just a bunch of box a box of  wires and then if you push it too far  you're like come on it's just a box of  wires so we were constantly riding this  line of of balancing the emotion and  then the other thing for me is is these  real humans these people who care so  um Doug has this great line in the movie  where he says you you only get this kind  of experience this kind of Adventure  once and I thought wow you know this guy  before I'd ever met him and Jennifer  they're gonna see this movie you know  with narratives those characters don't  exist so they're not never going to see  the movie but they're going to see this  movie so I there's a sense of uh  I don't know burden I guess  to make them experience the film how  they experience the adventure  yeah that's a lot of pressure now that I  think about it I mean it's still just  music so we like it no worries Mark I  love that you you